\f0\fs24 \cf0 ISSUES RESOLVED:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\li720\fi-720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}I found through various testing that the solution was not being updated. It was almost as if the original solution generated was acted upon once by Newton-Raphson, then completely ignored. It was cycling the same initial guess over and over again. I had Claude update the \'91nrDerivatives\'92 function and the \'91sparseApp\'92 file so that the changes generated by NR were actually reflected.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}There was an initial guess that was pretty bad. I think it was encoded in \'91firstStepSparseMB.hpp\'92, but even using the exact solution as a guess, it almost immediately diverged. Claude had said that there was something wrong with this and that the algorithm doesn\'92t even need it, since you can pass it right through to the NR algorithm. The function is still used so it is not entirely deprecated.\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Residual computation mismatches. There was a mix of use of relative and absolute residuals. The algorithm now specifically and only uses relative residuals. I would have never caught this. \
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}There was an issue that the \'91ops::threshold\'94 function never inserts any points, it just asserts that points exist. Once Newton evolves the field, we have to update where points are essential and where they are not. So, before each NR entry, \
\pard\tx940\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\li1440\fi-1440\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl1\cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8259 	}rebuilds the current field densely\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8259 	}re-discretizes the field based on the thresholding value\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8259 	}rebuilds the distribution\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8259 	}passes into NR algorithm \
\pard\tx220\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\li720\fi-720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 After these changes were made, the solution was actually converging given a \'93poorly informed\'94 initial guess (set to 0.1 on the interior - i confirmed that this is really the case). I ran this for a few trials where jMax = 4, nu = 0.05, for various epsilon values. Then, \
\pard\tx220\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\li720\fi-720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0\cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}jMax = 5 presented issues. Claude recommended I change the preconditioner to \'91BoomerAMG\'92 and this seemed to solve the problem. I confirmed that this solver produces the exact same results as the jMax=4 trials, so it is actually solving a problem and not just disguising another. Have yet to explore jMax=6 problems, so maybe this is just a bandaid. \
\pard\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 After this, I ran some trials with nu=0.01, so a much steeper problem. It threw a hissy fit, but\
\pard\tx220\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\li720\fi-720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0\cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}In the same vein as the Sod problem, I included a \'91coarsening\'92 tool that presents the initial solves as a much smoother curve. As it builds closer to a final solution, I turn the steepness up so it generates the results we want. This seems to work very well. Thank you, Cody.\
\pard\tx720\tx1440\tx2160\tx2880\tx3600\tx4320\tx5040\tx5760\tx6480\tx7200\tx7920\tx8640\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
