ISSUES IN PROGRESS:\
The main issue, according to Claude, is how the tangent is computed. Claude\'92s interpretation is that the tangent values computed at/near sparse points is incorrect and thus guiding us toward an incorrect solve direction. Here is its reasoning:\
\

\f1\fs28 \cf2 \'93
\f2\b \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
the tangent is built as if the dropped points don\'92t exist.
\f1\b0 \cf2  But they do \'97 they are filled in by interpolation from the kept points. So when Newton moves a kept point, the interpolated points near it move too, and the equation is being checked on those moved points as well. The direction-finder ignores that ripple, so its sense of \'93which way is downhill\'94 is wrong exactly where the grid is sparse.\'94\
\

\f0\fs24 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 Elementary explanation, but this is what it told me. \
\
So far, the solver works as expected for jMax=4. It seems to be off by an order of accuracy for jMax=5, regardless of the value of nu used. I have not tried jMax=6, but that is next on my list as soon as I finish writing this. This indicates to me that SOMETHING is correct - but something is also incorrect. I don\'92t think the residual at this point is wrong. The only thing not touched is the tangent. \
\
So it seems so far we might just be getting lucky with our tangent computation, that some values are so insignificant that the algorithm can blast its way through a suboptimal tangent and converge. But you can see in the printouts that the residual still does not really converge. It finds a good initial descent and then tends to hover around 1.3 or something like that. It doesn\'92t get smaller than that for more complicated problems. \
\
Given all of this, I tend to think that Claude might have some validity to it. I\'92ll need to investigate further, but for now, I am throwing as many test problems as possible at it to see where it faults. \
